Overlay patch 0045: game-side camera export for stereoscopic 3D
(= reference 0032 concept, MK64 sites; VISION-PRO-LUS-PLAYBOOK §2.5).
MK64 composes guPerspective x guLookAt COMBINED in the projection slot
(GameCamera::SetViewProjection — same shape as OoT's z_view), so 0044's
injector needs the camera basis exported from the game:
- gSoh3DCamRight/Fwd/Eye: basis+position from the player-1 camera's
  pos/lookAt/up at the exact values guLookAt consumed (fwd = normalized
  lookAt-pos; right = fwd x up, normalized).
- gSoh3DCamP00 = 1/(tan(fovy/2)*aspect) — matches guPerspective's [0][0]
  for the same fieldOfView (fovy degrees) and gScreenAspect.
- gSoh3DCamDist = |lookAt-pos|: adaptive-convergence anchor — the kart
  sits on the panel plane. NOTE MK64 world scale is smaller than OoT's;
  0044 keeps reference clamps until live camDist is measured (bridge 3d).
- Export sites: GameCamera::SetViewProjection (kart follow + look-behind,
  which subclasses without overriding) AND TourCamera::SetViewProjection
  (pre-race fly-by — virtual-dispatched INSTEAD of GameCamera, and its
  shot CUTS are the convergence-snap case). FreeCamera (debug) skipped —
  stale basis there is acceptable.
- Player-1 only (_camera->playerId == 0): split-screen render order is
  winner-dependent, and VP is single-player; belt-and-braces with the
  g3DInPlay gate below.
- Comfort flags from game_state_handler (runs every frame, every state):
  gSoh3DPaused = gIsGamePaused != 0 (holds pausing-player-index+1);
  gSoh3DInPlay = RACING && SCREEN_MODE_1P && !quit-transition. The
  ENDING podium and CREDITS stay flat (v1; perspective sites exist for
  later). No aiming export — MK64 has no first-person mode; look-behind
  is an instant cut handled as a convergence snap, not a profile.
REV2 (R6, VR first-person seat): the camera export alone cannot seat a
first-person view. MK64's racing camera aims at
`player->pos + R(yaw) * camera->unk_3C` (src/camera.c ~line 512) — a
target AHEAD of the kart, not the kart — so `camEye + camFwd*camDist`
lands in FRONT of the machine with the kart BEHIND the eye, which is
exactly what the user saw in the headset ("I looked behind me, I can see
myself (Toad)"). So rev2 additionally exports the PLAYER KART's own
pose from `gPlayerOne`, at the same player-1-gated site:
- gSohVRKartPos[3]  = gPlayerOne->pos
- gSohVRKartVel[3]  = gPlayerOne->velocity  (the seat suite's
  "neutral gaze faces the direction of travel" cross-check)
- gSohVRKartYawBam  = the kart's yaw in the CAMERA (atan2s) convention.
  REV3 (R8) NEGATES `gPlayerOne->rotation[1]` to get there. Rev2 shipped
  it raw on the strength of `cameras[id].rot[1] = gPlayerOne[i].rot[1]`
  (camera.c:1281) — which is a one-off GRAND-PRIX STAGING assignment,
  not the racing convention, and getting it wrong is the whole of
  the user's "joystick left, camera turns right" inverted-orbit bug.
  The ground truth is the physics, not an angle: func_8002AE38
  (player_controller.c:1115) advances the kart with
  `sins(-rotation[1]) * speed, coss(-rotation[1]) * speed`, and
  calculate_orientation_matrix's rot-Y (racing/math_util.c:616) maps the
  kart model's +Z onto (-sin(rot1), 0, cos(rot1)) — the same vector —
  while atan2s(x, z) (racing/math_util.c:900) returns the angle whose
  forward is (+sin, +cos). MIRRORED. One conversion, at this boundary.
- gSohVRKartValid   = 1 only while the GameCamera export site runs for
  player 1 (racing, 1P). TourCamera (fly-by) deliberately does NOT set
  it: there is no kart to sit in during a fly-by, and the shell falls
  back to the camera-derived seat.
The kart export is GameCamera-only, so it lives beside the shared basis
helper rather than inside it.
REV5 (R16-C, the rear-view pane): MK64's OWN look-behind camera is exported
from LookBehindCamera::Tick. It is a whole second Camera object created for
player 1 at spawn (src/spawn_players.c:1301) whose only difference from the
chase camera is the Z sign of both kart-local offsets
(src/engine/cameras/LookBehindCamera.cpp:26-30) -- same height, same pitch,
same FOV, same smoothing, same wall pushout -- and World::TickCameras ticks
it EVERY FRAME whether or not C-left is held (bActive is set in the ctor and
never cleared). So its pos/lookAt/up are live, warm and free, and 0044 can
build the pane's view from them for exact C-left parity instead of guessing
a mirror. Published as a SEQLOCK with a frame stamp, exactly like the kart
pose (rev4): the consumer is the 90-120 Hz compositor and the bridge, and a
validity flag whose false state is part of a normal frame is trap D40.
  gSohVRRearPos/At/Up  -- the look-behind camera's own eye, target and up.
  gSohVRRearSeq/Frame  -- the seqlock and the game frame it belongs to.
  gSohVRRearValid      -- 1 once a player-1 look-behind camera has exported.
REV5 also exports gSoh3DCamAspect (= gScreenAspect, 1.3333 in 1P racing).
The rear pane's framebuffer was sized off the EYE extent on a comment that
claimed the aspects matched; they do not, and the shell needs the game's own
number to size the pane's target correctly.
REV6 (R17-A item 1, THE GHOST DRIVERS): both position exports gain a PREVIOUS
value. The engine walks the display list N times per game frame at
FrameInterpolation_Interpolate(t), so every object it draws advances in
sub-frame steps -- but the VR seat is composed from THIS pose, exported once
per game frame, and therefore steps only at game-frame boundaries. Relative to
the seat a nearby kart alternates on-time / half-a-frame-ahead on every
present: a monocular, un-screenshottable, speed- and parallax-proportional
double image, which is exactly what the user reported on 1.0.0.13. The seat has
to be interpolated with the SAME t as the walk it is composed for, and that
needs the frame it is interpolating FROM.
  gSohVRKartPosPrev[3] -- the kart position exported for the previous game
    frame, published inside the SAME seqlock as the current one so the consumer
    gets a coherent PAIR or neither.
  gSoh3DCamEyePrev[3]  -- the chase camera's previous eye position, for the
    modes whose seat is the camera (third person, Diorama, and first person's
    no-kart fallback).
Nothing else changes: the shell differences them, the ENGINE applies the
difference scaled by the walk's own t (overlay 0044 rev13), and `vr set
seatinterp 0` restores 1.0.0.13 exactly.
REV7 (R17-C, from the R17-A code review): THE SEED. rev6 set prev = cur
UNCONDITIONALLY on every export, so the FIRST export after a discontinuity
paired this game frame's pose with a pose from whenever the site last ran --
the last frame of the previous race, the frame before a Lakitu respawn, the
frame before a menu. 0044 rev13's only protection was the 400-unit teleport
heuristic, and a race restart or a retry respawns on the grid, which is near
the finish line the previous export left the kart on: a delta UNDER 400 units
that is not a frame of travel at all, spent as a one-frame seat nudge of up to
400*(1-t). R16-A was a whole round about a one-frame seat jump reading as a
violent snap in the headset, so this is not cosmetic.
So each export site now remembers the game frame it last ran on and SEEDS
prev = cur -- a zero delta, no interpolation, exactly the rev5 behaviour for
that one frame -- whenever this export is the first of the process, the first
after `gSohVRKartValid` went 0 -> 1, or the first after a game-frame
discontinuity (`gSohVRGameFrame != last + 1`: a menu, a fly-by, a pause, a
restart, or the R16-A hole fault). The bookkeeping is a file-scope static per
TU, which is also correct across the GameCamera <-> TourCamera hand-over: a
fly-by cut is a discontinuity for the camera basis too.
  gSohVRSeatSeeds       counts the seeded exports (the "it is in the path" line).
  gSohVRSeatSeedFault   the RED CONTROL / fault injector. 0 = shipping.
    1 = rev6 exactly: never seed. N > 1 = never seed AND fabricate a stale prev
    N units away on the first export after a discontinuity, so the defect has a
    KNOWN magnitude instead of depending on where the last race happened to end
    (trap D11: an assert that can only NOT see something is worthless).
REV8 (R21 item 2, THE REAR PANE'S GHOSTS): the look-behind camera export gains
the same PREVIOUS value the kart and the chase camera got in rev6, seeded the
same way as rev7 (prev = cur on the first export of the process and after any
game-frame discontinuity), inside the same seqlock as the current pose so the
consumer reads a coherent pair or neither. the user on 1.0.1.19: the ghosts R18
removed from the forward view are still in the REAR-VIEW pane. The pane's view
was built around this export (game frame N) on every walk, while every object
in the list is drawn at FrameInterpolation's sub-frame t -- so the pane camera
stepped at game rate while the world glided, which is R17-A's ghost exactly,
and its pre-multiply made it worse (VR-R21-NOTES §2). 0044 rev16 interpolates
the pane camera along (prev, cur) with the walk's own t.
  gSohVRRearPosPrev[3] -- the look-behind camera's previous eye position.
  gSohVRRearSeeds      -- its seeded exports (own counter; rev7's is the seat's).

All exports write shell-owned globals (SohIosShell.m); extern "C" decls
at FILE scope in the C++ TUs (block-scope externs namespace-mangle — the
project's recurring linkage trap). __IOS__-guarded: the macOS build
builds this vendor and links no shell.

